I recommend running both HL7 and FHIR feeds in parallel, with a Mirth channel that fans out to both destinations. This will allow us to verify parity between the two systems before making a cutover.

Here's an example of how we could set up the Mirth channel:

```java
// Create a new Mirth channel
Channel myChannel = new Channel();

// Set up the HL7 inbound endpoint
myChannel.addInboundEndpoint(new HL7InboundEndpoint());

// Set up the FHIR outbound endpoint
myChannel.addOutboundEndpoint(new FHROutboundEndpoint());

// Fan out to both destinations
myChannel.addRoute(new Route("HL7", "FHIR"));
myChannel.addRoute(new Route("FHIR", "HL7"));

// Log any discrepancies between the two systems
myChannel.addLogger(new Logger());
```

By running both feeds in parallel, we can ensure that our FHIR resources are correctly generated and validated before making a cutover.

Ah, great point! Custom Z-segments can be a challenge. There are a few options to consider:
* Use FHIR extensions to persist the data that needs to remain in your system. For example, you could create an extension on the Patient or Encounter resource to store your custom routing information.
* Store the Z-segment data in message headers/meta instead of as part of the FHIR payload. This way, the data is still accessible but doesn't clutter up your FHIR resources.
* Take a hybrid approach and create a custom namespace for your Z-segments within your FHIR server. This would allow you to keep the data separate from the main FHIR resources.

